## Step 1: Define the Initial Scene
The animation starts with a circle of diameter D displayed on the screen. This circle represents the initial state before any transformation.

## Step 2: Animation of Circle Unfolding
The circle then "unfolds" or transforms into a straight line. This unfolding should be animated in a way that clearly shows the circumference of the circle being stretched out into a linear form.

## Step 3: Display the Unfolded Length
Once the circle has been fully unfolded into a straight line, the length of this line (which represents the circumference of the original circle) is measured and displayed. This length should be shown to be approximately π times the diameter (πD) of the original circle, emphasizing that it is slightly more than 3 times the diameter (since π is approximately 3.14159).

## Step 4: Visual Comparison
To drive the point home, a visual comparison can be made by placing three diameters of the circle end-to-end alongside the unfolded circumference. This comparison will visually demonstrate that the circumference of the circle (now in its linear form) is indeed longer than three times the diameter of the circle.

